X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.203
In this lesson, you will learn what a coding agent is,

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.972
how it reasons through tasks and how it uses tools

3
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.775
like code execution and file system to complete them.

4
00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:10.877
Let's dive in.

5
00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.445
This is an example of a coding

6
00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.180
agent that we will build in this course.

7
00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:18.084
Here, the agent turns your request into a working application.

8
00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.487
To achieve that, it writes code files,

9
00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:23.490
executes them, and instantly serves a web app.

10
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.592
all on its own in the cloud.

11
00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.995
An AI agent can simply be defined as an LLM

12
00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.297
calling Tools in a Loop with Context.

13
00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:34.000
Tools can be code that the agent runs

14
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.603
or external services that it can call.

15
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.572
For example, working with files, searching the web,

16
00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.575
or visualizing data insights.

17
00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:45.245
The context is any input for the LLM.

18
00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:48.248
It can be System Instructions, Tools Definitions,

19
00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.250
User Message, or more.

20
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.752
The LLM, based on the context,

21
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.754
decides if and which tool to call.

22
00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:56.956
The agent calls and runs the tool.

23
00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.392
appends the results to the context

24
00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.095
and repeats the loop until the task is completed.

25
00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.098
An AI agent might just reason for a while,

26
00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:07.400
browse its resources and return an answer.

27
00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.569
You can ask the agents to

28
00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.604
do tasks like booking flight for you

29
00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:15.508
or doing research on a subject and writing a summary on it.

30
00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:19.813
But then, there are agents like Gemini CLI, Claude Code or Cursor.

31
00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.782
These are coding agents that write and run code

32
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:26.486
and related tasks like generating a long script, debugging it,

33
00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:29.489
or editing dozens of files in a project.

34
00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:32.992
That requires code execution in a controlled environment

35
00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.195
to iterate on the code over and over.

36
00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:38.498
Coding agents also need filesystem access.

37
00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:42.502
And because coding is complex, most tasks need long-running sessions

38
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.004
to install dependencies, compile, run tests,

39
00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.006
patch errors, and retry.

40
00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.809
Finally, these agents need stronger security.

41
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.013
Untrusted code must be handled such that experiments don't break your machine

42
00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:57.417
or malicious actors don't gain access to your system.

43
00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:59.819
Like any other agent, your coding agent

44
00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.721
will have an LLM at its core.

45
00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:04.524
The question is, how do we pick the right one?

46
00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:07.560
First, the model should support function calling.

47
00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:11.064
That means that the LLM is provided with a set of tools

48
00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:13.533
and it can decide when and how to use them.

49
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.835
Second, the context size matters.

50
00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:18.838
Research shows that around 30k tokens of context

51
00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:22.542
is the first point where agents can reliably handle real-world tasks.

52
00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:25.845
It corresponds to about 40 to 50 pages of text,

53
00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.548
which is like a medium-sized GitHub repo.

54
00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:32.552
This is needed because working with larger projects like Django or PyTorch

55
00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:35.355
will require a model to read and edit many files,

56
00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:38.158
easily using tens of thousands of tokens.

57
00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:41.628
Third, for the right decision, we can evaluate the LLMs,

58
00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:43.930
ideally with coding specific benchmarks.

59
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.332
One of the most widely used is SWE-bench.

60
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:49.636
It tests whether a model can take a real GitHub issue,

61
00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:52.539
generate a patch and pass the repo's unit tests.

62
00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:56.009
Here is the leaderboard from October 2025.

63
00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.711
But remember that this is just one possible metric.

64
00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.015
You can evaluate models depending on your use case.

65
00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:04.317
Context is everything the LLM sees.

66
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:07.287
For coding agents, context can be the system prompt,

67
00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:10.990
the external input like a PDF or a database search result,

68
00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.294
the user prompts, tools, and tool execution results.

69
00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:17.463
and other things like current branch commit,

70
00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.166
file summaries, previous patch notes,

71
00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:23.870
dependency versions, or short-term memory facts from earlier runs.

72
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.873
Context Engineering is the art of keeping the context small

73
00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:29.676
while providing the most relevant information to the model

74
00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.444
to complete the current task.

75
00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:33.446
We need to manage context length

76
00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:37.150
because especially for coding agents, it can grow very quickly.

77
00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:41.621
Every edit run debug cycle adds more text to the conversation.

78
00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:44.123
Tool outputs can be massive,

79
00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.626
especially when they return big JSONs or logs.

80
00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.729
Then you can get repeated error stacks on top of that.

81
00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.232
The result is what we call context rot.

82
00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:54.867
As the token count grows,

83
00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:58.071
the model starts paying less attention to what matters and quality drops.

84
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.573
We just saw how context can explode.

85
00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:04.877
In coding agents, one common source of tokens is raw tool outputs.

86
00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:06.713
The first way you can mitigate

87
00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:08.514
that problem is by keeping tool outputs

88
00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.516
small, consistent, and structured.

89
00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:13.119
For example, we have a tool called get_weather

90
00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.521
that will ping an API to get the results.

91
00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:18.124
If we just return the response's data,

92
00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:21.327
we might overwhelm the context with information that's not needed.

93
00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:23.529
So you can parse the output

94
00:04:23.529 --> 00:04:25.932
and return only the data the LLM actually needs.

95
00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.701
This doesn't apply only to JSON responses.

96
00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:30.937
Logs can grow huge too, so we

97
00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.405
clip them to the last few lines

98
00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:35.208
and drop repeated messages to keep the context small.

99
00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.111
Coding agents need to scan the file system quite often.

100
00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:41.180
For example, listing files based on some pattern.

101
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.182
But if you work on a large project,

102
00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:45.385
a directory may contain hundreds of files.

103
00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:48.254
And returning all of them can really flood the context.

104
00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.757
One common strategy is to paginate the results

105
00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:52.425
and give the model the ability

106
00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:54.360
to request the next page if needed.

107
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.130
We can also filter the listing first.

108
00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.333
For example, only Python files or only certain directories.

109
00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:02.735
So the model sees just what matters.

110
00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:06.639
Your coding agent will need specific tools to accomplish its coding tasks.

111
00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:09.242
At least running code and accessing the file system.

112
00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.644
To run the code, we should follow some rules.

113
00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:15.815
I think code generated by an LLM should be treated as untrusted.

114
00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.950
We should try to avoid running

115
00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.886
the code actions directly on the host.

116
00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:22.121
Ideally, we should go for a safer

117
00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.390
environment isolated from the rest of the system.

118
00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:26.793
We can also enforce strict limits

119
00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:29.095
on the CPU, memory, and execution time.

120
00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.364
So a runaway process can't

121
00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:35.168
bring things down. We can also block network and filesystem access

122
00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.136
unless the task explicitly needs it.

123
00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:39.706
And finally, we keep the model on track

124
00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:41.841
by pointing it to our chosen tech stack.

125
00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:44.043
Otherwise, it can start hallucinating library

126
00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.346
names or using tools you don't want.

127
00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:50.149
Coding tasks require reading, editing, and writing files.

128
00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:53.019
So the agent needs accessing the file system.

129
00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:55.588
One good practice is to have strict permissions

130
00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:59.992
and allowing the agent to work only inside a specific working directory.

131
00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.261
To search files, we can equip the

132
00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:04.297
agent with a regex based file search.

133
00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.800
To search inside a specific file, we can use fuzzy matching.

134
00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:11.704
That way, the agent can look for relevant content or patterns,

135
00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:14.707
even when there isn't an exact match to the search query.

136
00:06:15.108 --> 00:06:18.211
So an AI agent can be defined as an LLM using tools

137
00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:19.979
to perform actions in the loop

138
00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:21.948
and to accomplish the given task.

139
00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:26.152
The loop is just whatever is going on between two user messages.

140
00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.855
Most tasks need multiple rounds of reasoning,

141
00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:31.257
so the model calls tools again and again.

142
00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:34.260
Sometimes it can call the same tool repeatedly.

143
00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.329
But we need to know when to stop.

144
00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:40.433
There must be an exit condition. Agents are not perfect.

145
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.568
They can fail or might spin out

146
00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.537
of control while trying to recover errors.

147
00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:47.140
They can require evaluation of the results,

148
00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.642
either by human in the loop or an LLM.

149
00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.945
The longer and more complex task you assign to your agent,

150
00:06:53.546 --> 00:06:55.548
more prone it will be to make errors.

151
00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:58.651
Tracking errors systematically is critical

152
00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:02.155
because your agent might get stuck trying to fix an error forever.

153
00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:05.057
To prevent it, we can keep track of errors.

154
00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.660
For example, having a maximum retry counter.

155
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.328
We can ask the user to

156
00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:10.963
provide input and help with the errors.

157
00:07:11.164 --> 00:07:14.267
We can also help model by clustering errors together,

158
00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.769
removing the old ones while still keeping

159
00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:18.871
the context clear.

160
00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:20.339
You also need to choose the

161
00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:22.074
environment where coding agents operate the best.

162
00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:25.278
Cursor uses local execution, some agents use Docker,

163
00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:28.948
and some like Lovable, AInauts, Perplexity use sandboxes.

164
00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.551
When deciding about the right environment,

165
00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:34.754
we should think of security, like attack surface,

166
00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:37.256
and whether we are exposing our operating system.

167
00:07:37.657 --> 00:07:39.692
Then there is developer experience.

168
00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:42.895
We want to avoid code workarounds and make users happy.

169
00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:45.765
We also want our agents to have customized environments

170
00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:49.635
to use variety of languages, tools, and pre-installed packages.

171
00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:52.138
There needs to be a good maintenance tool,

172
00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:54.106
reduce any downtimes of the infrastructure

173
00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:55.808
the agent is running on,

174
00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:58.811
to provide reliability and good support for end users.

175
00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:02.215
And finally, we want the environment to start fast.

176
00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:04.750
And we need to do all this at scale

177
00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.752
and be ready for our agent to serve

178
00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.554
even millions of end users.

179
00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.156
In the next lesson with Francesco,

180
00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:11.757
you will apply all these concepts

181
00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:15.628
to build your first simple coding agent. All right, see you there.
